    "body": "## Problem\n\n`lsp/robotcode.lua` puts the active venv's `site-packages` into `$PYTHONPATH` so that a globally\ninstalled `robotcode` can see the project's Robot Framework packages (#4367). The path it globs is\n`<venv>/lib/python*/site-packages`, and multiple hits are joined with `:`. Both are Unix-only.\n\nA Windows venv has no `lib/python<version>/` directory at all — `python -m venv` puts everything\nunder `Lib/site-packages`:\n\n```\n$ python -m venv proofvenv && ls proofvenv\nInclude  Lib  Scripts  pyvenv.cfg\n$ ls proofvenv/Lib\nsite-packages\n```\n\nSo the glob matches nothing, `cmd_env.PYTHONPATH` ends up as the empty string, and the venv support\nis silently a no-op on Windows. And even with a correct path, `:` is not the path separator there.\nThe CI only runs `ubuntu-latest`, so nothing catches this.\n\n## Proof\n\nI dropped a marker module into a real Windows venv's `Lib/site-packages`, then ran a plain\ninterpreter (standing in for the `robotcode` process, which runs outside the venv) with the\n`$PYTHONPATH` each version of the code produces:\n\n```\nglob('lib/python*/site-packages') -> []                                  # what the current code looks for\nglob('Lib/site-packages')        -> ['.../proofvenv/Lib/site-packages']  # where it actually is\n\nbefore: import FAILED (PYTHONPATH='')\nafter:  import OK -> installed in the venv\n```\n\nAnd the separator, with two `site-packages` dirs in `$PYTHONPATH`:\n\n```\nsep [:]: FAILED with PYTHONPATH='.../Lib/site-packages:C:/.../sp2'\nsep [;]: both imports OK\n```\n\n## Solution\n\nGlob `Lib/site-packages` and join with `;` on Windows, keep the existing behaviour everywhere else.\nI also run the venv path through `vim.fs.normalize()`, because `$VIRTUAL_ENV` on Windows comes back\nwith backslashes and the pattern is concatenated with `/`.\n\nUnix behaviour is unchanged: same pattern, same `:` separator, and `glob()` still returns `''` when\nthere is no venv layout to find, exactly as before.\n\nFound by a defect-hunting pipeline I build and run ([Dev-next-gen](https://github.com/Dev-next-gen)), using Claude Code with Anthropic's Claude Opus 5.\n",
